  "summary": "Mexican strawberry shipments are under US investigation on antidumping grounds, with timing sensitivity tied to the US election cycle. The Mexican civil aviation authority will undergo US FAA assessment this week on safety standards compliance, with potential consequences for Mexico's aviation category ranking. Official gazette publication of reforms to Mexico's Manual de Contabilidad Gubernamental establishes new accounting and reporting standards for federal entities.",
  "lead": {
    "h1": "Mexican strawberry exports face US antidumping investigation ahead of US elections",
    "context": "Mexican strawberry shipments are under US investigation on antidumping grounds, with timing sensitivity tied to the US election cycle.",
    "background": "Mexico shipped US$853m of strawberries to the United States from January 2025 to June 2026, and the US Commerce Department set a preliminary antidumping margin of 18.32% in March 2026.",
    "view": "The preliminary 18.32% dumping margin from the US Commerce Department threatens Mexican strawberry exporters, whose shipments to the United States totaled $853 million from January 2025 to June 2026. CNA director Haro says the review is tied to the 2026 US midterms, not trade violations, since Florida's growers carry outsized political weight despite California dominating supply.",
    "prediction": "The next milestone is the US Commerce Department's final antidumping determination, following March's preliminary 18.32% margin. A higher final duty would confirm election-year pressure shaped the case, while a lowered margin would support Mexico's push for continued market access before the midterms.",
    "analysisV": 8,
