      "headline": "Mexico's Treasury reforms governmental accounting manual",
      "context": "Official gazette publication of reforms to Mexico's Manual de Contabilidad Gubernamental establishes new accounting and reporting standards for federal entities.",
      "background": "Mexico's Consejo Nacional de Armonización Contable sets the Manual de Contabilidad Gubernamental under the 2008 Ley General de Contabilidad Gubernamental, the rulebook all federal, state and municipal entities must use to record and report public finances.",
      "view": "A reform to the accounting manual changes reporting rules, not spending itself, so it does not by itself add or cut fiscal space. Its practical effect depends on whether it improves visibility into obligations like Pemex support, which already strains a fiscal position with debt near 53.2% of gross domestic product in 2025.",
      "prediction": "The reformed manual takes effect as federal entities apply it in coming reporting cycles, alongside the same-day reform to the Conceptual Framework of Governmental Accounting. Confirm adoption by checking whether the next quarterly financial statements follow the new criteria.",
      "analysisV": 8,
