      "headline": "AT&T and Telcel may be barred from intelligent networks spectrum auction due to spectrum concentration",
      "context": "Mexico's communications regulator has proposed excluding carriers holding more than 20 percent of spectrum in PCS, AWS, 2.3 GHz, 2.5 GHz, 3.3 GHz, and 3.5 GHz bands from accumulating additional spectrum in the intelligent networks auction.",
      "background": "Mexico's Comisión Reguladora de Telecomunicaciones is designing the CRT-RRI-01 auction for spectrum serving industrial radiocommunications, covering sites like mines, ports and oil fields, under the US-Mexico-Canada Agreement Article 18.20.4, which requires telecom spectrum processes to consider competition and market approaches.",
      "view": "A 20% national spectrum cap would exclude Telcel, at 33.51%, and AT&T, at 26.21%, the only two carriers with national mobile networks, leaving smaller players to win the auction. AT&T argues this reserves the band for third parties and departs from the market-based approach the US-Mexico-Canada Agreement's telecom article requires, a dispute regulators must resolve before bidding begins.",
      "prediction": "The CRT must finalize the CRT-RRI-01 auction rules after public consultation, deciding whether to keep the 20% cap that excludes Telcel and AT&T. If it survives, AT&T's US-Mexico-Canada trade agreement Article 18.20.4 challenge could escalate into a formal trade dispute.",
